/**
 * @mainpage BinaryTree API Documentation
 *
 * @section intro Overview
 *
 * <b>BinaryTree</b> is a header-only C++20 binary tree with two properties
 * usually kept apart:
 *
 * - <b>Speed</b>: nodes live inline in one contiguous slot array — no
 *   per-node heap allocation, cache-friendly walks.
 * - <b>Safety</b>: node references are generational handles, not raw
 *   pointers — stale, foreign, and out-of-range references are detected
 *   and rejected instead of dereferenced.
 *
 * Single header: @c include/binary_tree/binary_tree.hpp. Namespace
 * @c binary_tree. No dependencies beyond the C++20 standard library.
 *
 * @section quickstart Quick start
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <binary_tree/binary_tree.hpp>
 * #include <iostream>
 *
 * int main()
 * {
 *     binary_tree::BinaryTree<int> tree;
 *
 *     if (auto st = tree.EmplaceRoot(10);
 *         !std::holds_alternative<std::monostate>(st))
 *     {
 *         return 1; // never ignore mutation results
 *     }
 *     auto root = tree.Root();
 *     if (!root)                       // never use handles unchecked
 *         return 1;
 *
 *     tree.EmplaceLeft(root, 5);       // check in production code
 *     tree.EmplaceRight(root, 15);
 *
 *     tree.InOrder([](const int &v) { std::cout << v << ' '; });
 *     // prints: 5 10 15
 *
 *     if (auto left = tree.Left(root); left)
 *         if (int *pv = tree.Value(left))
 *             *pv = 6;                 // handle -> value
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section safety Safety problems this design solves
 *
 * Classic raw-pointer node trees fail in ways that are silent until they
 * crash. This library removes each failure mode structurally:
 *
 * - @b Dangling @b node @b pointers after @b growth or @b erase.
 *   Pointers into a moving container (or a freed node) are undefined
 *   behavior on use. Here handles address slots by 32-bit index —
 *   storage growth relocates nodes, handles keep resolving. @see
 *   binary_tree::BinaryTree::IsValid
 *
 * - @b Stale @b handles @b after @b slot @b reuse. A handle to a recycled
 *   node would alias unrelated data. Every slot carries a 64-bit
 *   generation; recycling bumps it, so old handles fail validation.
 *   Generation wraparound is blocked — a retired slot is never silently
 *   resurrected. @see binary_tree::TreeError
 *
 * - @b Cross-tree @b handle @b mixups. Passing a handle from tree A to
 *   tree B would corrupt or read the wrong storage. Each handle embeds
 *   its tree id; mismatched ids are rejected (@c InvalidParent, @c
 *   nullptr, @c false — no undefined behavior).
 *
 * - @b Out-of-bounds @b slot @b access. Every resolve bounds-checks the
 *   index before touching storage.
 *
 * - @b Silently @b ignored @b failed @b insertions. Functions that
 *   "return bool you forgot to check" hide bugs. Every mutation returns
 *   a status variant carrying a specific @ref binary_tree::TreeError
 *   (@c RootAlreadyExists, @c InvalidParent, @c ChildAlreadyExists).
 *
 * - @b Accidental @b copies @b / @b double @b frees. Copy construction
 *   and copy assignment are deleted; ownership moves explicitly and
 *   @c noexcept. Self-move-assignment is a safe no-op.
 *
 * - @b Dangling @b value @b pointers. Value() pointers follow
 *   `std::vector` reallocation rules and the contract is documented at
 *   the API; handles are the always-safe path, and Reserve() removes
 *   reallocation entirely when the final size is known.
 *
 * - @b Broken @b move-only @b payloads. Slot storage relocates nodes
 *   with moves only — types such as @c std::unique_ptr work (covered by
 *   tests), no default construction required.
 *
 * All of the above is enforced by 28 unit tests including a storage
 * contiguity audit, a 10 000-node growth audit, and move-only
 * reallocation sweeps, run under AddressSanitizer and UBSan.
 *
 * @section contracts Contracts and requirements
 *
 * - @b Type @b requirements: @p T must be move- or copy-insertable.
 *   Move-only supported, default-construction not required.
 * - @b Pointer @b lifetime: pointers from Value() die when an insertion
 *   grows storage; handles never die from insertion. Reserve(count)
 *   freezes growth for known sizes.
 * - @b Threading: concurrent const usage (traversals, Value(), IsValid())
 *   is safe; any mutation needs exclusive external access to the tree.
 * - @b Errors: no exceptions from library logic; failures arrive as
 *   return values. Only @p T construction and allocation can throw.
 *
 * @section pitfalls Pitfalls — what to avoid
 *
 * Bad handles never cause undefined behavior here — but they fail
 * calls, and failed calls do nothing. Watch for these traps:
 *
 * @warning Always check handles before use. Root(), Left(), and
 * Right() return a null handle when the tree is empty, the child
 * slot is empty, or the parent handle is stale or foreign. An
 * unchecked handle passed onward produces an error result or a null
 * pointer — the program keeps running while your logic silently
 * skips work. Test with `if (handle)` or @c IsValid(handle) first.
 * @see binary_tree::BinaryTree::IsValid
 *
 * Insertions fail just as quietly:
 *
 * @warning Always check insertion results. EmplaceRoot(),
 * EmplaceLeft(), and EmplaceRight() return a status variant.
 * Ignoring it hides @c RootAlreadyExists, @c InvalidParent, and
 * @c ChildAlreadyExists — the classic "insert failed but my code
 * kept going" bug. Branch on the result:
 * `std::holds_alternative<std::monostate>(status)`.
 *
 * Raw pointers are the second trap:
 *
 * @warning Never keep a pointer from Value() across insertions.
 * Pointers follow `std::vector` reallocation rules: a growing
 * insertion invalidates every outstanding pointer. Re-fetch after
 * mutations, or call Reserve() up front when the final size is
 * known. Handles are never invalidated by insertion — prefer them
 * as the long-lived reference. @see binary_tree::BinaryTree::Value
 *
 * Threading is your responsibility:
 *
 * @warning Never mutate the tree while other threads read it. There
 * is no internal locking: a concurrent traversal plus an insertion
 * is a data race. Const operations (traversals, Value(), IsValid())
 * may run in parallel; any mutation needs exclusive external
 * access.
 *
 * Handles live inside one process only:
 *
 * @attention Never persist or send handles. Tree ids come from an
 * in-process counter — a handle is meaningful only inside the
 * process (and tree) that issued it. Do not write handles to disk,
 * put them in files, or compare them across processes or runs.
 * Persist values or your own indices instead.
 *
 * Cross-tree mixups are detected, not fatal:
 *
 * @note A handle from another tree is rejected, not undefined
 * behavior — but receiving one means a caller bug. Check which
 * tree issued the handle; foreign handles fail IsValid() with a
 * specific error, never a crash.
 *
 * Ownership is explicit:
 *
 * @note Move, don't copy: copy construction and copy assignment are
 * deleted. After a move, previously issued handles keep resolving
 * inside the new owner (the tree id travels with the data); the
 * moved-from tree receives a fresh id, so its old handles die.
 * Self-move-assignment is a safe no-op.
 *
 * Throwing constructors leave nothing half-built:
 *
 * @note Exceptions leave the tree intact. Only @p T construction
 * and allocation (`std::bad_alloc`) can throw; if they do, no link
 * is written and Size() does not move. At worst one unused empty
 * slot remains — traversals skip it.
 *
 * Capacity only grows:
 *
 * @note Reserve() never shrinks. Capacity may exceed Size() for
 * the tree's lifetime; that wastes nothing observable. Pointers
 * fetched before Reserve() may die if Reserve() itself reallocates
 * — re-fetch once after it.
 *
 * @section commands Building and generating docs
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * make setup       # dependencies (vcpkg) + Debug build
 * make test        # unit tests (ASan/UBSan)
 * make benchmark   # Release benchmarks
 * make docs        # this documentation (Doxygen via vcpkg)
 * @endcode
 *
 * Cross-platform (Windows/macOS included), same CMake targets:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake --preset debug
 * cmake --build --preset debug
 * ctest --preset debug
 * cmake --build --preset debug --target docs
 * @endcode
 *
 * Doxygen itself is installed automatically by vcpkg through the
 * project overlay port @c ports/doxygen — no manual tool setup.
 *
 * @section arch Storage architecture (why it is fast)
 *
 * @code
 * m_Slots:       [ optional<Node<T>> | optional<Node<T>> | ... ]  contiguous
 * m_Generations: [ u64                | u64                | ... ]  parallel 1:1
 * m_FreeSlots:   recycled indices (prepared for future deletion)
 *
 * NodeHandle = { u32 index, u64 generation, u64 tree_id }
 * @endcode
 *
 * Resolution is index arithmetic plus one load — no pointer chase to a
 * separately allocated node. Traversal streams pure node data; the
 * generation array stays out of the hot path and is touched only by
 * validation.
 */
